  $t4$Raising Pigs in Kenya: A Shamba Space Guide$t4$,
  $s4$A practical, Kenya-focused guide to keeping pigs -- from choosing breeds and building suitable housing to feeding, breeding, piglet care, and protecting your herd from African Swine Fever and other disease risks -- plus how to find reliable buyers and think through the real costs of the enterprise.$s4$,
  $c4$## Getting Started

Pigs are one of the most land-efficient livestock options for Kenyan smallholders. They need less space than cattle, reproduce faster than most farm animals, and can turn locally available feed resources -- kitchen leftovers, crop residues, and farm by-products -- into a marketable product. Kenya's pig sector ranges from a single sow kept in a backyard in Western Kenya or Nyanza, through small and medium commercial units in Central Province, Rift Valley, and around Nairobi, to large integrated operations that breed, raise, slaughter, and process pigs under one roof. There is no single "right" way to keep pigs -- the right system depends on your land, capital, labour, and access to markets.

Before bringing home your first pig, think honestly about what you can offer: secure housing, reliable feed and clean water, time for daily care, and access to a vet or livestock extension officer. Farmers who invest in breeding records, feeding, and hygiene consistently end up with healthier animals than those who leave pigs to fend for themselves. Start small and grow your herd only as your management capacity grows with it.

## Choosing the Right Breed

Most pigs raised commercially in Kenya are exotic breeds or their crosses, principally the Large White, the Landrace, and, to a lesser extent, the Duroc. The Large White is widely regarded as robust, adaptable, and a strong performer, and is the preferred breed among many Kenyan pig farmers. Landrace pigs are also commonly used for cross-breeding but tend to need more attentive management and better feeding, doing best under close confinement rather than free-ranging. Duroc genetics are valued for growing and finishing well under tropical conditions and are often used as a terminal sire in cross-breeding programmes aimed at faster growth and better carcass quality.

Away from the organised breeding sector, many pigs found in free-range and backyard systems in areas such as Western Kenya and Nyanza are the product of generations of uncontrolled cross-breeding between exotic types, with no formal local breed as such. These animals have adapted to scavenging and low-input conditions but are frequently highly inbred, since a single boar is often shared informally among many neighbouring farms with no organised breeding plan -- a documented cause of poor fertility, slow growth, and small mature size.

Two practical takeaways: source breeding stock from a different bloodline than boars already circulating on neighbouring farms, to avoid compounding inbreeding, and be aware that most formal buyers and slaughter facilities in Kenya prefer white-skinned exotic crosses -- animals with black or mixed skin colouring can attract a lower price at major slaughter points. Buy from a reputable breeder, established commercial farm, or recognised institution, and ask about an animal's parentage before you buy.

## Housing and Farm Setup

Housing quality in Kenya varies enormously by system, and this variation tracks closely with disease outcomes: free-range backyard pigs with no housing experience the poorest hygiene and highest disease risk, small-scale commercial units with concrete floors washed daily do noticeably better, and large commercial farms with dedicated cleaning resources do best of all. Even on a small budget, the principle holds: a floor that can be cleaned (concrete or another washable, well-drained surface) and pens that separate animals by age and stage -- piglets, weaners, growers, pregnant sows, nursing sows, and boars -- will do more for your pigs' health than almost anything else you invest in.

Kenyan law requires anyone planning to keep pigs to obtain a license from their county livestock office, and a veterinary officer typically inspects the premises to confirm the housing meets basic requirements, including rodent-proofing, before a license is issued. Talk to your county veterinary or livestock office before you build, so your plan is approved from the outset.

Good housing gives shelter from sun, rain, and wind, drainage so pens do not become waterlogged, and enough space for pigs to move and separate when needed. Plan for manure early: Kenyan regulation requires it to be composted for at least a month before use on crops, and manure dumped at the roadside or into drains is a recognised source of environmental and neighbour conflict.

## Feeding and Water

Feed is consistently the largest recurring cost in Kenyan pig production. Commercially raised pigs are typically fed different rations by life stage -- a starter/"creep" feed for piglets, a general sow-and-weaner type feed for weaners, sows, boars, and gilts, and a finisher feed for pigs being fattened, usually offered twice daily. Exact quantities should match your pigs' age, weight, and condition; ask a feed supplier or a livestock extension officer to help you work out an appropriate feeding plan rather than relying on a fixed formula.

Common local feed ingredients include maize and its milling by-products (bran), wheat and wheat bran, rice bran, cotton seed cake and other protein sources, with limestone commonly used as a mineral source. Many smallholders reduce feed costs using crop residues and farm by-products -- sweet potato vines, cassava, kales, cabbage, Napier grass, sugarcane tops, garden weeds, and fruit and vegetable peelings. Balanced nutrition matters at every stage: growers need adequate energy and protein, while breeding sows and boars have their own needs to stay fertile and in good condition. A vet or extension officer can help judge whether your feeding programme meets these needs.

Some farmers, especially near schools, hotels, or institutions, supplement with swill or kitchen waste. Be candid with yourself about the risk: the safety, quantity, and nutritional quality of swill is never guaranteed, pigs fed on unbalanced waste-based diets gain weight poorly, and uncooked waste of unknown origin can spread disease, including diseases that have historically moved through pork and pig products in Kenya. Treat swill as a supplement, not a complete diet, and discuss safe practice with a vet, particularly in areas with a history of African Swine Fever.

Pigs also need constant access to clean water, ideally available at all times rather than rationed -- water matters for digestion and for coping with Kenya's warmer regions. Keep drinking points free of algae, manure contamination, and stagnant residue, since dirty water is both a welfare issue and a route for disease and parasites.

## Breeding

Pigs have a comparatively short reproductive cycle, one reason they suit smallholders with limited land -- under good management a sow can potentially farrow more than once within a year, though this depends on nutrition, health, and how quickly she is rebred after weaning. Exact gestation length, breeding intervals, and litter size targets vary by breed and management, and are best discussed with a vet or extension officer rather than assumed from a fixed number.

The single biggest breeding problem documented in Kenya's smallholder sector is inbreeding, driven by boars being informally shared among many neighbouring farms with no record-keeping, and by farmers sometimes having no real alternative because unrelated boars are scarce or costly. This is directly linked to infertility and poor reproductive performance. Rotate or replace boars periodically, know the parentage of animals you breed, and avoid repeatedly mating close relatives -- a local pig farmers' group can help members share access to unrelated boars.

Sows need a clean, dry, sheltered space to farrow, away from other pigs and protected from cold or draughts, since chilling is a major cause of early piglet loss. Many free-range sows in Kenya farrow in the open with no special preparation, which is associated with heavy piglet mortality -- a stark illustration of why a properly prepared farrowing space is worth the investment, however small.

## Piglet Care

The first days and weeks carry the highest risk of loss: piglet mortality has been reported as high as 50 to 80 percent where sows farrow with no special care, largely from starvation, chilling, and disease. This argues for a few basic, low-cost practices: make sure piglets nurse from their mother soon after birth, keep the farrowing area clean, dry, and warm, watch for piglets being pushed away from the sow or failing to gain weight, and keep a nursing sow and litter separate from other pigs until the piglets are stronger.

Many farmers identify piglets soon after birth, for example by ear-notching, to track parentage and support breeding and sales records -- worth adopting even on a very small farm. In commercial farrow-to-weaner enterprises, piglets are commonly weaned and sold onward at around six to eight weeks old; in less intensive traditional systems, weaning and sale often happens later, around two to three months. Avoid moving or selling very young, underweight, or visibly unwell piglets, and let newly weaned piglets settle onto solid feed before further stress such as transport. For concerns about piglet growth, scouring, or ill health, consult a vet or extension officer promptly.

## Hygiene and Biosecurity

Biosecurity deserves special emphasis in Kenya because of the ongoing risk of African Swine Fever (ASF), a viral disease that has caused repeated, serious outbreaks in the country's pig populations. There is no vaccine and no cure for ASF -- prevention through biosecurity is the only real defence.

Wild pigs -- warthogs and bush pigs found in many parts of Kenya -- are recognised carriers of the ASF virus, and contact between domestic and wild pigs is a documented infection route, along with a soft tick species that can carry the virus between animals. The disease has also spread between areas through movement of pork and pig products, which is why buying pigs of unknown health status, feeding uncooked waste of unknown origin, and allowing your pigs free contact with animals from elsewhere all raise your risk.

Practical steps that fit any scale: keep pigs confined rather than free-ranging or scavenging, since these systems carry the highest documented disease risk in Kenya; quarantine any newly bought pig before mixing it with your herd, and ask about its health history and origin; limit visitors' and vehicles' contact with your pigs, and have people clean footwear and hands before and after handling animals; obtain the health certification and movement permits Kenyan regulation requires for moving pigs between locations; and isolate any sick pig immediately. Kenyan law also places a duty on pig keepers to report notifiable diseases to a government veterinary officer -- do this promptly if you see unexplained illness or unusual deaths in your herd.

## Disease Prevention and Parasite Management

Beyond ASF, Kenya's pig sector has recorded pneumonia, diarrhoea, and mastitis in nursing sows on commercial farms, and porcine cysticercosis -- a parasite that infects pigs which pick up tapeworm eggs from human faeces where sanitation is poor or latrines are not used. Cysticercosis matters beyond animal health because it is zoonotic: people can develop serious illness, including brain infection, from eating raw or undercooked infected pork -- one more reason pork should be properly inspected and thoroughly cooked. This risk is documented as particularly relevant in parts of Western Kenya where free-ranging pigs and limited latrine use coincide, though good hygiene and confinement reduce it anywhere. Foot and mouth disease has also affected pigs on mixed cattle-and-pig farms in Central Kenya, usually following cattle outbreaks, and can trigger movement restrictions that disrupt sales.

External parasites -- mange, lice, and ticks -- are common in free-range systems, and jiggers have also been documented affecting pigs, notably in Busia County; internally, gut worms and lungworm are widely reported in free-range pigs. Good hygiene, dry bedding, and less contact with contaminated soil and standing water all help. One caution: some commercial farmers in Central Province have been found deworming monthly, a frequency linked to dewormers becoming less effective over time (resistance). Rather than deworming on a fixed schedule of your own choosing, ask a vet or extension officer to design a parasite programme suited to your farm and region.

For all health issues, the same principle applies: hygiene, good nutrition, confinement, and quarantine reduce disease risk far more reliably than reacting afterwards. When you see off-feed animals, lameness, skin lesions, breathing difficulty, scouring, sudden deaths, or unusual behaviour, contact a qualified veterinary or livestock extension officer for diagnosis and a farm-specific plan rather than guessing or self-medicating.

## Handling, Welfare, and Records

Kenyan law places a general welfare duty on pig keepers to maintain a clean environment, feed animals adequately, provide clean water at all times, and call a qualified vet when pigs are sick. Calm, routine handling makes pigs easier and safer to manage. Prolonged tethering, common in some traditional systems, has been linked to wounds where ropes chafe the neck and legs; if you must tether pigs seasonally, check them daily and adjust ropes. Proper housing is preferable to prolonged tethering for both welfare and disease control.

Simple records pay off at any scale: which sow was bred to which boar and when, farrowing dates and litter sizes, piglet identification, weaning dates, any illness or treatment given (and by whom), and sales. Records help you avoid inbreeding, identify your better breeding animals, plan ahead of farrowing, and build a track record that can help with buyers, cooperatives, or credit. Farmers observed keeping clean, well-fed pigs with breeding and sales records also attracted more buyer interest.

## Marketing

Marketing pigs in Kenya works differently from other livestock: unlike cattle, sheep, or goats, there are generally no open live pig auction markets. Pigs are traded directly -- from farm to traders and brokers who move between farms, to local butcheries, to slaughter slabs, or under contract to processors. Some of Kenya's largest formal buyers do not purchase live pigs on the open market; farmers who supply them, including contract farmers, transport their own pigs to the factory and are paid according to dressed weight after slaughter -- an arrangement worth understanding fully before committing to it.

Kenya also has a range of dedicated pig slaughter facilities of varying standards, some licensed and regularly inspected with separate clean and dirty areas, others simple open-air slaughter slabs. A growing number of smaller processors buy carcasses to turn into value-added products such as sausages and bacon, offering another route to market. Some communities use informal direct-to-consumer sales, where a farmer takes neighbours' orders ahead of slaughtering a pig.

Two realities worth planning around: buyers generally prefer white-skinned exotic-type pigs, with black or mixed-coloured and underweight animals fetching less at slaughter points; and market access is uneven across the country, with some regions lacking nearby slaughter facilities, forcing long-distance transport that adds cost and stress. Before scaling up, research the buyers and facilities realistically available to you, and consider joining a farmers' association or cooperative -- several exist in Kenya specifically to help pig farmers organise, share market information, and strengthen their bargaining position.

## Common Mistakes

A national review of Kenya's pig sector found fewer than half of the country's pig farmers were practising proper housing, feeding, and healthcare -- basic good practice, not expensive inputs, is often the biggest gap. Recurring problems include: free-ranging and scavenging, linked to the highest disease and injury rates of any system studied; relying on a single, informally shared boar, which drives inbreeding and infertility; underfeeding or feeding unbalanced waste-based diets with no supplementation; neglecting manure and sanitation, which creates both animal-health and neighbour problems; deworming on a fixed, frequent schedule rather than professional advice; and moving or selling pigs without regard for the health certification and movement rules that may apply. Avoiding these patterns puts a farm well ahead of much of the sector, even without large capital investment.

## Farm Business Considerations

Before investing, think through the main cost categories: housing and site infrastructure, breeding stock (and its ongoing replacement to avoid inbreeding), feed -- by far the largest recurring cost in Kenyan pig production -- labour, veterinary and extension support, and transport to market. Mortality, particularly among piglets, is a real financial risk that good housing, feeding, and hygiene substantially reduce, though never eliminate; build this into your planning.

Access to capital and credit has been flagged as a genuine constraint for many Kenyan pig farmers, as has demand that is sometimes uneven relative to the number of pigs being produced in an area -- scaling up without first confirming a buyer is a risk, not a guarantee of income. On the positive side, pigs offer land efficiency compared with cattle, a shorter production cycle than most livestock, and an expanding range of market outlets. Farmer groups and cooperatives, including national and local pig farmers' associations, have been organised specifically to help members access better markets, share information, and sometimes jointly organise services such as feed milling. Whatever your scale, keep clear records of costs and income so you can judge, season by season, whether your particular enterprise is working, rather than relying on general assumptions about what pig farming can earn.

